details.contract:
goldens.rs locates the built binary (CARGO_BIN_EXE_sangkar), and for every rust/tests/golden/<fixture>/<tool>.json:
1. builds the fixture database into a temp dir with the TYPESCRIPT binary - this phase's promise is that the Rust server serves a TypeScript-built graph - using ~/.bun/bin/bun run bin/sangkar.ts index --repo-root rust/tests/parity/fixtures/<fixture> --data-dir <temp>;
2. runs <bin> <tool> <args from the golden> --json --repo-root <fixture> --data-dir <temp> with SANGKAR_CURSOR_KEY set to the golden's fixed key;
3. compares stdout bytes to the golden's stdout and the exit code to exit_code, with the same structural comparison as scripts/parity/compare.py implemented in Rust (key sets, values, array order) so a single field difference names its JSON pointer;
4. compares the next token byte for byte - that is the cursor contract, and it is what forces every per-tool argsHash to be right.
When the TypeScript binary is absent (after phase_5 deletes it) the fixture database is built with the Rust indexer instead, and the SAME byte comparison runs. That remains sound only because TASK-048 pins the six ordered row sequences: ids and order must match or the goldens break. Note that dependency in the test header, and never let the test silently skip: it must always compare every golden and print the count.
A golden captured before a payload field existed is a stale golden: the test fails and the fix is recapture, not relaxation. Mark the broken fixture's unreadable-directory case with an explicit skip guarded by a runtime file-mode check.
Update rust/tests/parity/README.md with the exact commands, the fixture list, and a paragraph on why the cursor key is fixed and why goldens are committed.

Port `src/shared/cli/index-gate.ts` (123 lines).
```rust
pub enum GateAction { Run, Ask, Refuse(RefuseReason) }
pub enum RefuseReason { Unregistered, TooDeep }
pub fn index_gate(input: &GateInput) -> GateAction;
pub fn refusal_message(input: &GateInput) -> String;
pub const CONTINUE_QUESTION: &str = "continue with this repo? (y/N) ";
pub fn ask_continue(input: &mut impl BufRead, output: &mut impl Write) -> bool;   // only /^y(es)?$/i proceeds
```
The five facts the gate reads are the ones the TypeScript version reads: whether `--repo-root` was given explicitly, how many levels the autodetection climbed, whether the tree is registered, whether `.sangkar`/`.git` was found, and whether stdin is a terminal. `--yes` skips the question and acts as `Run` for the `Ask` action; a `Refuse` action is never overridable.
`refusal_message` is printed to stderr and the process exits 2; the question is written to stderr and the answer read from stdin, so stdout stays clean for the rendered envelope.
Tests port `tests/cli-index-gate.test.ts`: all five facts' combinations, `y`/`yes`/`Y`/`n`/empty answering, `--yes` bypassing the question, a `Refuse` not bypassed by `--yes`, and the exit-2 path with the message on stderr.

Port the store-lifecycle half of `src/index.ts`: `FileFingerprint` (:290), `fingerprintOf` (:315), `sameFile` (:332), `openStore`/`refreshStore` (:438-533), `dbSizeBytes` (:1003).
```rust
pub struct FileFingerprint { pub path: PathBuf, pub inode: u64, pub size: u64, pub mtime_ns: i128 }
pub fn fingerprint_of(path: &Path) -> Result<Option<FileFingerprint>>;   // None when the file is absent
pub fn same_file(a: &FileFingerprint, b: &FileFingerprint) -> bool;      // inode + size + mtime, never path equality
pub struct Store { /* reader handle, fingerprint, schema_version, db_size_bytes */ }
pub enum StoreFailure { Missing, SchemaTooNew { found: i64, built: i64 }, Open(String) }
pub fn open_store(data_dir: &Path) -> Result<Store, StoreFailure>;
pub fn refresh_store(store: &mut Store) -> RefreshOutcome;               // Reopened | Unchanged | Missing
```
same_file MUST compare the inode, not the path: the publish step replaces the inode, and a path comparison is exactly the bug this module exists to prevent (copy the comment). On `Reopened`, the reader handle, the schema version and db_size_bytes are replaced atomically from the caller's point of view.
A missing database is a `bootstrapping` answer, not an error; a database whose `meta.schema_version` exceeds the built one is `AppError{code:"schema_too_new"}` with a hint naming the indexer version; db_size_bytes comes from the file metadata at open time.
Tests port `tests/store-refresh.test.ts`: a publish (rename a new file over the live one) is detected and the reader answers the NEW generation; an untouched file reports `Unchanged` in under 1 ms; a deleted file reports `Missing`; a database with a bumped `schema_version` is refused with `schema_too_new`; and two distinct files with the same size are not `same_file`.
